  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ attains its maximum value.\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(1)={0}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${0}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={0}^{\u2218}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its maximum values at\n\n$$\nx={360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{\u2218}<x\\le {180}^{\u2218}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={0}^{\u2218}$ is the only solution.",
